table FailureTools: "Failures And Fixes"
    columns:
        failure: "What went wrong"
            "The failure as an author would describe it."

        cause: "Why it happens"
            "What is really going on."

        tool: "Reach for"
            "The Doctrine tool that fixes the cause."

        example: "See"
            "One shipped example."


document GettingGoodResults: "Getting Good Results"
    "Load this first. It teaches how to get agents that stay small, stay in step, and do well on work nobody wrote down. The other references teach the language. This one teaches what the language is for. Start here when an author says what they want or what went wrong. Go to a language reference once you know which tool fits."
    section purpose: "What Doctrine Is For"
        "An agent does well on a case nobody wrote down when it understands what matters there. It knows what is at stake, who pays when things go wrong, and what only looks important. A list of steps can't give it that. A list covers the cases on the list, and the next case is never on it."
        "Doctrine can't write that understanding for you. It does three plainer jobs, and those jobs decide whether the understanding survives. It keeps each idea in one place, so a fix reaches everyone who holds it. It gets each idea to the agents who need it, and no further. And it shows you what a change reached before anything ships."
        "So do your own thinking on the words, and let the compiler carry them. An author who works the other way ends up with a tidy tree of typed declarations that teach nothing."
        "A clean `doctrine check` proves the text compiles and your load rules hold. It doesn't prove the text teaches. The Loop, at the end of this file, says how to find that out."
        "`examples/193_small_team_end_to_end` shows the whole method on one small team. Read it once before you build your own."
    section failures: "Which Failure, Which Tool"
        "Start from what went wrong, not from a feature. Each row names a failure authors really meet."
        table failure_tools: FailureTools
            rows:
                copied_role:
                    failure: "Two roles say nearly the same thing"
                    cause: "Someone copied one and changed a few words. Each fix must now be made twice, and one gets missed."
                    tool: "One shared role, with `values` for the words that differ"
                    example: "`examples/173_late_bound_values`"
                wrong_reach:
                    failure: "A lesson reached agents it doesn't fit, or missed ones it does"
                    cause: "It sits at the wrong level of the tree."
                    tool: "The narrowest team that holds everyone who needs it. Add `requires load` or `forbids load` when a miss costs a lot"
                    example: "`examples/180_home_tree`, `examples/186_rule_load_assertions`"
                long_home:
                    failure: "A home is too long"
                    cause: "Worked cases and procedure sit in always-on text."
                    tool: "`details` for the cases, and `budget` to see the limit coming"
                    example: "`examples/181_details`, `examples/183_load_budgets`"
                split_contract:
                    failure: "Two agents disagree about what a handoff holds"
                    cause: "Each side wrote down its own idea of it."
                    tool: "One `output` with a `Message` target, which both homes render"
                    example: "`examples/191_message_outputs`"
                no_source:
                    failure: "Nobody can say where a claim came from"
                    cause: "The words outlived the person who made the call."
                    tool: "`quote`, then `doctrine sources` to list who said what"
                    example: "`examples/190_quotes`"
                base_drift:
                    failure: "A base says something that is wrong for one member"
                    cause: "The base grew past what its members share."
                    tool: "`drop` with a reason. If several members drop the same part, split the base into the craft they share and the job only some do"
                    example: "`examples/169_drop_with_reason`, `examples/193_small_team_end_to_end`"
                quiet_change:
                    failure: "A line that matters changed and nobody who cared heard"
                    cause: "Nothing ties the line to the person who cares."
                    tool: "`person` with `owns:`. Then `doctrine diff` names the change"
                    example: "`examples/189_person_and_ownership`"
                frozen_markdown:
                    failure: "A big Markdown home nobody dares touch"
                    cause: "Nothing shows what an edit would change."
                    tool: "`doctrine import`, then share one piece at a time"
                    example: "`examples/192_doctrine_import`, `examples/179_markdown_backed_declarations`"
        "If the failure isn't here, ask what it costs to leave it alone. Many one-off failures need a better sentence and no new construct."
    section one_fix: "One Fix Lands Once"
        "Drift starts with a copy. The day you paste a sentence into a second role, you have two sentences, and nothing says they must agree. Someone fixes one. The other stays wrong and sounds just as sure. Nobody finds out until an agent acts on the old one."
        "So a sentence many roles need lives in one role, and the words that differ come from `values`. In `examples/173_late_bound_values`, one QA role reads `bet` on one team and `feature` on another. Fix the sentence once, and both homes change."
        "Sharing has its own failure, and it's harder to see. Force two things to share when they only look alike, and every later change to one bends the other. A few signs tell you it has happened:"
        """
- A value keeps growing. A word or a short phrase fills a blank in a shared sentence. A whole sentence says the line was never shared.
- A role has many blanks, and each team fills in all of them differently. That is two roles wearing one name.
- A sentence is true for one team. It belongs in that team's override. Don't add a value so the other teams can leave it empty.
- Several members drop the same part of a base. The base is wider than what they share.
"""
        "Two texts that differ for a real reason aren't copies. Ask whether a fix to one would also be right for the other. `doctrine check` warns about near copies with `W301`, as `examples/188_project_warnings` shows. Treat the warning as a reason to look, not a verdict."
    section lessons: "Where A Lesson Lands"
        "Most of the life of a doctrine tree is lessons. Something went wrong, and someone wants it not to happen again. How you land them decides whether the tree stays small or turns into a pile."
        "A correction usually arrives as a rule: never do this, with a date and a name. That tells you what someone saw. It isn't the line to add. Ask what an agent would have had to understand to see this coming, then look for the text that should have taught it."
        "First, use `doctrine where \"some words\"` to find the source of a sentence and the homes it reaches. Change that text so it would have caught this case and still handles the old ones. A good landing leaves a file about as long as it found it, and the reader meets the lesson inside an idea that was already there."
        "A new paragraph beside the old one is how drift starts inside a single file. The next agent has two instructions to square, and no way to tell which one won. Add a new idea only when nothing in the tree is about this."
        "Then pick the level. Teams form a tree, and each level is read by everyone under it. Too low, and you'll write the lesson again for the next team, which is a copy. Too high, and every agent pays to load a lesson that isn't theirs, and some will act on it where it doesn't fit. Ask who would have gone wrong without this, and put it at the lowest level that holds all of them."
        "Last, run `doctrine diff`. It lists which homes each change reaches. Read the size beside each declaration: a lesson added inside an idea adds little, while a new paragraph stands out. Check who the change reaches too. If a lesson for one pod reaches every home, it landed too high. `examples/187_doctrine_diff` shows the report."
        "Load rules are for the few lessons where a miss costs a lot. `requires load` says a home must carry something, and `forbids load` says it must not, even through a chain of shared roles. See `examples/186_rule_load_assertions`. Don't write one per lesson. A rule can prove the text arrived. It can't prove anyone understood it, and each one is one more thing to keep true."
    section thin: "Keep Always-On Text Thin"
        "Everything in a home is read at the start of every session, on every job, whether the job needs it or not. The cost is more than tokens. Each added line dims the lines around it, and agents get worse with each special case they must steer around. Some runtimes also cut a long file without saying so. The agent then works from part of it, and nobody is told."
        "So a home holds what an agent needs on most turns: the idea, why it matters, and the one case that shows where the answer turns. Say it the way an expert would say it to a smart newcomer. If it takes a paragraph, the incident is probably still in there. The date, the story, and the names say how you learned it. They don't teach the reader."
        "Move worked cases and step-by-step procedure to `details`, as in `examples/181_details`. A good split passes one test. An agent that reads only the home still gets the idea right, and opens the details for the hard case. If the home makes no sense without its details, the idea moved too."
        "A shared role's cases can use each team's words through values. A team can add its own case with `details work.plan.local:`. An override keeps cases written in the inherited body. Use `drop` with a reason when a case no longer fits. See `examples/181_details` and `examples/182_shared_details`."
        "Leave out what an agent sees by looking: folder layout, the frameworks in use, what a linter already enforces. It will look anyway, and your copy of those facts goes stale."
        "`budget` and `doctrine load` show how close each home is to its limit, counting the parent files it reads. See `examples/183_load_budgets`. Read the limit as a sign the home says too much, not as a target. Deleting the one case that teaches, just to fit, is worse than running over. Ask what an agent would get wrong without this line. If you can't name it, cut the line."
    section prose_or_types: "Prose Or Types"
        "Ask one question of each thing you want to say: could the right answer differ from case to case?"
        "If it can't, it's a fact, and the compiler should hold it: routes, schemas, who receives a message, what a role loads. A typed fact can't drift. A prose copy of it can, so don't restate what a type already owns."
        "If it can, it's judgment, and it stays prose. What makes a review good, when to stop and ask, which shortcut will cost more later. Turn that into fields and you get a form. The form gets filled in, and the thinking stops."
        "Both mistakes leave signs. A schema with fields no reader uses is too much type. A paragraph a later owner must parse to find a route is too little."
        "A `Message` shows the line between them. In `examples/191_message_outputs`, what a message carries is written once and rendered in both homes, so sender and receiver can't disagree. The body is still prose, written to be read cold, and Doctrine won't let you give it a `shape`."
        "Provenance works the same way. In `examples/190_quotes`, who said something and when are typed, and the words stay the words. Use a `quote` when the authority matters: an owner's call that no reasoning would produce, or a decision nobody could work out again. A quote doesn't replace the reason. \"Never do this (the owner, June 3)\" says where a rule came from, not what it protects."
        "`person` with `owns:` in `examples/189_person_and_ownership` keeps the list of who cares about what in one place, away from the text itself. An edit to a line can't quietly delete its owner. Keep the list short. It's for lines where a quiet change would do harm."
    section bad_patterns: "Patterns That Go Bad"
        "A few shapes compile fine and cost you later. Each one starts as a reasonable move."
        """
- Values used as templating. A value is a word that differs, as in `examples/173_late_bound_values`. When values start to build sentences or pick between behaviors, you have a small template language with no tests. Doctrine has no conditions on purpose. Give that team an override.
- Deep inheritance. Each level is one more place a reader must look to learn where a line came from. `doctrine where` finds it, but a reviewer still has to hold the chain in their head. `examples/193_small_team_end_to_end` stays shallow. If a parent exists for one child, fold it in.
- Piles of `drop`. One `drop` with a reason records a real difference, as in `examples/169_drop_with_reason`. Several on one member say it isn't that role. Split the base.
- Restating what the harness owns. Words about tools, scheduling, or memory go stale the day the harness changes. Put the rule where the harness can own it, as `examples/118_output_target_delivery_skill_binding` does for delivery.
"""
    section porting: "Port Before You Refactor"
        "An old Markdown home is full of lessons someone paid for, and some messy parts are messy for a reason. So move it across in an order that loses nothing, one file at a time."
        "Start with `from`, which composes the Markdown as it stands, as in `examples/179_markdown_backed_declarations`. The runtime gets the same text, and links follow the file on their own. When you want to edit the text in Doctrine, run `doctrine import`. It prints `.prompt` source, and `--check` shows the render still matches. See `examples/192_doctrine_import`. Imported text doesn't follow its file, so first rewrite relative links as paths from the repository root."
        "Only then start to share: roles first, then values, then rules and budgets. Take one step at a time, and read `doctrine diff` after each. Port and rewrite in one go, and nobody can tell what changed. Before you cut a messy section, ask what it was protecting, and keep that."
    section the_loop: "The Loop"
        "Every change goes round the same way. Edit the text. Run `doctrine diff`, and read who it reached. Run `doctrine build`. For changes to judgment, run the agent linter in change mode. For changes to shared judgment, give a fresh agent the home and a case the home never mentions, plus one where the obvious move is wrong, and watch what it does."
        "The first steps prove the text got where you meant it to go. Only the last one shows that it teaches. `examples/187_doctrine_diff` shows the report that starts the loop."
    section current_practice: "Read Current Practice"
        "This field moves fast, and this file will age. Before you write for a runtime, read what is current:"
        """
- The host's own authoring skills, when it has them, such as `agents-md-authoring`, `prompt-authoring`, `skill-authoring`, and `intuition-building`.
- Claude Code's memory docs.
- The Agent Skills spec at agentskills.io.
- The AGENTS.md guidance at agents.md and in OpenAI's Codex docs.
"""
        "When they disagree with this file on how to write for a runtime, trust them. When they disagree on what Doctrine does, trust this file and the shipped corpus."
